const QUESTIONS = [{"id":1,"topic":"intro_ml","topicName":"ML Fundamentals","question":"A major European airline wants to identify distinct groups of frequent flyers based on booking patterns, flight routes, spending, and loyalty tier usage — without any predefined segment definitions. The marketing VP asks a junior data scientist to build a logistic regression model for this task. The VP's request reveals a fundamental misunderstanding. The specific paradigm mismatch AND the correct approach category are:","options":["A) Logistic regression can only handle numeric features, but booking patterns and flight routes contain categorical data that cannot be encoded numerically without losing essential information about the segments","B) Logistic regression requires a minimum sample size of 10 observations per feature to produce stable coefficient estimates, and the airline's feature count likely exceeds this threshold given the number of route combinations","C) Logistic regression produces linear boundaries between groups, which cannot capture the complex nonlinear patterns in multi-dimensional booking behavior that the airline needs to identify meaningful customer segments","D) Logistic regression requires predefined labeled categories as target output, but this task has no predefined segments, making it an unsupervised problem; clustering methods like K-means or DBSCAN are appropriate instead","E) None of the above"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The core issue is paradigm mismatch: discovering groups WITHOUT predefined labels is unsupervised learning. Logistic regression is supervised — it needs labeled target categories. K-means, DBSCAN, or hierarchical clustering are appropriate. B is wrong: logistic regression handles categorical features via encoding. C describes a real limitation but misses the fundamental issue. D invents a sample size rule."},
{"id":2,"topic":"intro_ml","topicName":"ML Fundamentals","question":"A hospital has labeled patient records with diagnoses and wants to (1) predict disease risk and (2) discover hidden symptom patterns without using labels. A colleague says both tasks are supervised since labels exist in the dataset. Why is this flawed?","options":["A) The hospital should use semi-supervised learning which automatically determines when to use labels and when to ignore them based on the statistical properties of each feature","B) Having labels in the dataset does not force every task to use them — task two deliberately ignores the labels to discover hidden structure, making it unsupervised learning","C) Disease prediction should use reinforcement learning where sequential patient outcomes serve as the reward signal, not supervised learning with static diagnostic labels","D) Unsupervised methods require datasets that contain absolutely no label columns in the data storage format, so the labels must be physically deleted before running task two","E) None of the above"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The presence of labels doesn’t force all tasks to be supervised. Task 1 uses labels (supervised), task 2 deliberately ignores them (unsupervised). B mischaracterizes reinforcement learning. C is wrong — unsupervised methods can run on datasets containing label columns. D describes a real technique but doesn’t address the logical flaw."},
{"id":3,"topic":"intro_ml","topicName":"ML Fundamentals","question":"Per the scikit-learn algorithm cheat sheet from the course: 100,000+ labeled samples, predicting a category, Linear SVC did not work. What does the flowchart recommend next?","options":["A) Abandon the classification framing entirely and reframe the problem as a regression task, predicting a continuous probability score for each possible category independently","B) Switch directly to a deep neural network architecture since the dataset is large enough to support the parameter count needed by modern deep learning model architectures","C) Revert to the KNeighbors classifier which makes no distributional assumptions about the data and typically works well when linear methods fail on complex classification tasks","D) Apply kernel approximation techniques first, and if those also underperform, move to ensemble classifiers that can capture more complex nonlinear decision boundary patterns","E) None of the above"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The scikit-learn cheat sheet flow: for >100K samples, try SGD/Linear SVC first, then kernel approximation, then ensemble classifiers. B skips intermediate steps. C (KNN) is recommended for smaller datasets. D is a misunderstanding — predicting class probabilities is still classification."},
{"id":4,"topic":"intro_ml","topicName":"ML Fundamentals","question":"A company detects fraud using well-defined, stable rules that rarely change. An executive pushes for an ML replacement. A senior engineer argues against it. Who is making the stronger technical argument?","options":["A) The executive is right: ML always outperforms rule-based systems because it can discover additional hidden patterns that hand-crafted rules will inevitably miss over time","B) The engineer is right: when patterns are well-defined and stable, rule-based systems are simpler, more transparent, and easier to maintain without adding ML complexity","C) The executive is right: current regulatory requirements mandate that all financial fraud detection systems must incorporate machine learning components for compliance purposes","D) The engineer is right: ML fundamentally cannot be applied to fraud detection because the severe class imbalance between fraudulent and legitimate transactions prevents learning","E) None of the above"],"correct":1,"explanation":"When rules are clear and stable, ML adds unnecessary complexity, opacity, and maintenance burden without benefit. B is false as a universal claim. C is wrong — ML handles class imbalance through various techniques. D is fabricated — no such regulation exists."},
{"id":5,"topic":"linear_regression","topicName":"Linear Regression","question":"A real estate model: price = θ0 + 350·area − 15000·bedrooms. A colleague claims the negative bedroom coefficient proves that adding bedrooms decreases home value. Is this interpretation correct?","options":["A) Yes: the coefficient directly tells us that each additional bedroom reduces the property value by exactly fifteen thousand dollars regardless of all other model features","B) No: negative coefficients in multiple regression always indicate measurement error in the corresponding feature rather than reflecting any genuine causal or associative relationship","C) No: the negative sign is an artifact of the gradient descent optimization algorithm converging to a local minimum rather than reflecting the true underlying data relationship","D) No: area and bedrooms are correlated, so the coefficient shows the effect of adding bedrooms while holding area constant — same area but more bedrooms means smaller rooms","E) None of the above"],"correct":3,"explanation":"In multiple regression, coefficients represent marginal effects holding other variables constant. Since area and bedrooms are correlated, more bedrooms at the same area = smaller rooms, which may decrease value. B is wrong — negative coefficients can be meaningful. C ignores the ceteris paribus interpretation. D is wrong — MSE is convex for linear regression."},
{"id":6,"topic":"linear_regression","topicName":"Linear Regression","question":"You have 500 samples and 10,000 features. A colleague suggests the Normal Equation for linear regression. What is the primary computational concern with this specific dataset?","options":["A) The Normal Equation requires loading all feature columns into memory simultaneously, which is infeasible when there are 10,000 columns regardless of available system resources","B) The Normal Equation assumes all features are uncorrelated with each other, which is extremely unlikely to hold with 10,000 features, causing numerically unstable coefficient estimates","C) The Normal Equation requires inverting a 10,000×10,000 matrix with O(N³) complexity, making the computation prohibitively expensive when the feature count is this large","D) The Normal Equation can only handle datasets where the sample count exceeds the feature count, so it would fail entirely and produce undefined results on this dataset","E) None of the above"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The Normal Equation computes θ = (XᵀX)⁻¹Xᵀy, requiring inversion of a features×features matrix. At 10,000 features, that’s O(10¹²) — computationally prohibitive. B is partially true (XᵀX may be singular) but pseudoinverse handles it. C conflates assumptions. D is misleading — the bottleneck is matrix inversion, not loading."},
{"id":7,"topic":"linear_regression","topicName":"Linear Regression","question":"A degree-15 polynomial is fit to 50 data points. Training MSE is 0.001 while test MSE is 45,000. A colleague says polynomial regression is inherently nonlinear and therefore cannot generalize. Is this diagnosis correct?","options":["A) No: the model underfits because a degree-15 polynomial is not flexible enough to capture the true signal, and increasing the degree further would reduce the test error","B) Yes: polynomial features above degree five are numerically unstable and produce unreliable coefficient estimates regardless of how much training data is available to the model","C) No: the model overfits because degree-15 has too many parameters for 50 points, memorizing noise; polynomial regression is still linear in its coefficients and parameters","D) Yes: polynomial terms like x² and x³ fundamentally change the optimization landscape, creating multiple local minima in the cost function that prevent finding good solutions","E) None of the above"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Polynomial regression IS linear in coefficients (it’s linear regression with transformed features). The problem is classic overfitting — too many parameters for too few points. B is wrong: MSE remains convex. C is absurd given near-zero training error. D overgeneralizes about numerical stability."},
{"id":8,"topic":"linear_regression","topicName":"Linear Regression","question":"Degree-4 and degree-5 polynomials both achieve R²=0.861. A simpler exponential model achieves R²=0.854. A colleague recommends degree-5 since it has the most parameters. Why is this recommendation problematic?","options":["A) Adding a parameter that contributes zero R² improvement only increases overfitting risk without benefit, while the simpler exponential model performs nearly as well with less complexity","B) R² values cannot be meaningfully compared across different model families because each family type defines explained variance using a fundamentally different baseline calculation method","C) The degree-5 polynomial introduces multicollinearity among the polynomial terms like x and x² and x⁵, which completely invalidates the R² metric for model comparison purposes","D) Nonlinear models like the exponential function always generalize better than polynomial models when training R² values are similar because they impose stronger structural priors on the data","E) None of the above"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Zero R² gain from degree 4→5 means the extra parameter adds overfitting risk without benefit. By Occam’s razor, prefer the simpler exponential (R²=0.854, nearly as good). B is a misconception — R² uses the same baseline. C is wrong about invalidation. D overgeneralizes."},
{"id":9,"topic":"linear_regression","topicName":"Linear Regression","question":"Feature ‘income’ ranges 20,000–500,000, ‘age’ ranges 18–80. Gradient descent converges very slowly. A colleague suggests switching to the Normal Equation to avoid the scaling issue entirely. Is this the best fix?","options":["A) The colleague is correct: switching to the Normal Equation is always preferable to gradient descent when convergence is slow because it is strictly more computationally efficient","B) Neither approach will work because features with vastly different scales violate the assumptions of linear regression, and the model must be entirely reformulated from scratch","C) The Normal Equation is the best fix because it finds the exact analytical solution in one step, while gradient descent with any learning rate produces only approximate results","D) The Normal Equation sidesteps scaling but is suboptimal — feature scaling makes gradient descent converge fast and remains practical for any dataset size unlike the Normal Equation","E) None of the above"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Feature scaling (standardization/normalization) makes GD converge fast by creating circular contours. The Normal Equation avoids this but is O(N³) for features — impractical for large feature sets. B is wrong: GD with scaling reaches the same solution. C is false. D is wrong for large feature counts."},
{"id":10,"topic":"linear_regression","topicName":"Linear Regression","question":"Surgery duration model: duration = 45 + 0.8·age + 12·complexity − 5·minimally_invasive. For a 60-year-old patient, complexity=3, minimally invasive surgery. What is the predicted duration in minutes?","options":["A) 128 minutes, calculated as 45 + 48 + 36 − 5 = 124 but with a standard clinical rounding correction factor of positive four minutes added to the final prediction","B) 130 minutes, calculated as 45 + 0.8(60) + 12(3) − 5(0) since minimally_invasive equals zero when the procedure actually uses the minimally invasive surgical technique","C) 88 minutes, calculated as 45 + 0.8(60) − 12(3) + 5(1) since higher complexity reduces duration while the invasiveness indicator adds time to the procedure estimate","D) 133 minutes, calculated as 45 + 0.8(60) + 12(3) + 5(1) since the minimally invasive indicator should be added rather than subtracted in the final prediction calculation","E) None of the above"],"correct":4,"explanation":"Correct: 45 + 0.8(60) + 12(3) − 5(1) = 45 + 48 + 36 − 5 = 124 minutes. No option matches. A invents a rounding correction. B incorrectly sets minimally_invasive=0 when the patient IS having minimally invasive surgery (should be 1). C reverses signs. D adds instead of subtracting."},
{"id":11,"topic":"gradient_descent","topicName":"Gradient Descent","question":"Batch gradient descent on a linear regression problem shows the cost oscillating wildly without converging. A colleague claims the oscillation proves there are local minima in the MSE cost function. Is this valid?","options":["A) No: MSE for linear regression is convex with a single global minimum; the oscillation is caused by a learning rate too large, which overshoots the minimum on each iteration","B) Yes: even convex functions can develop local minima when the number of features exceeds a certain threshold, which causes the observed oscillation pattern in the cost values","C) No: the oscillation is perfectly normal behavior for batch gradient descent and will eventually settle down after enough iterations regardless of the learning rate value chosen","D) Yes: while pure MSE is convex, the presence of feature interactions and polynomial terms in the data creates a non-convex landscape with local minima causing oscillation","E) None of the above"],"correct":0,"explanation":"MSE for linear regression is ALWAYS convex — one global minimum, no local minima, regardless of feature count. Oscillation = learning rate too large. B and D are incorrect about convexity. C is wrong: an overly large learning rate can cause divergence, not eventual convergence."},
{"id":12,"topic":"gradient_descent","topicName":"Gradient Descent","question":"A neural network converges to loss=0.45 (local minimum) instead of the global minimum at ~0.15. Switching from batch GD to SGD sometimes reaches 0.20. Why does SGD help, and does it guarantee the global minimum?","options":["A) SGD is mathematically guaranteed to find the global minimum given enough iterations because the random sampling eventually explores the entire parameter space exhaustively over time","B) SGD implicitly applies L2 regularization through the gradient noise, which smooths the cost function landscape and eliminates the local minima that trapped batch gradient descent","C) SGD’s noisy single-sample gradient estimates can knock the optimizer out of shallow local minima, but it provides no guarantee of finding the global minimum of the cost function","D) SGD computes more accurate gradients by focusing on one sample at a time instead of averaging across the entire noisy dataset, leading to more precise optimization steps overall","E) None of the above"],"correct":2,"explanation":"SGD’s noise is a feature for non-convex problems — it can bump the optimizer out of shallow local minima. But NO guarantee of global minimum (C is wrong). B inverts reality: SGD has LESS accurate gradients. D is creative but incorrect — noise doesn’t eliminate local minima."},
{"id":13,"topic":"gradient_descent","topicName":"Gradient Descent","question":"10 million samples. A colleague chooses SGD (batch size 1) over mini-batch GD (batch size 256), arguing SGD is faster since it processes less data per step. Who is making the better engineering decision?","options":["A) The colleague is correct: SGD with batch size one always converges faster than any mini-batch size because it performs more parameter updates per epoch of training data processed","B) The colleague is correct: SGD’s noisy gradients serve as implicit regularization, so it not only trains faster but also produces models that generalize better on unseen test data","C) Neither is optimal: batch gradient descent using all ten million samples per step would converge in the fewest total steps, making it fastest despite the per-step computation cost","D) Mini-batch is better: it benefits from GPU parallelism and produces more stable gradient estimates, making it converge faster overall despite processing more data per step","E) None of the above"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Mini-batch (e.g., 256) balances: stable gradient estimates, GPU parallelism, and practical convergence speed. SGD is noisy and doesn’t parallelize well. B ignores convergence quality. C is wrong: batch GD on 10M samples per step is too slow. D overstates SGD’s benefits."},
{"id":14,"topic":"gradient_descent","topicName":"Gradient Descent","question":"In the ‘Mount Errorest’ lecture analogy, you’re in dense fog on a mountain, can only feel the local slope, and take very tiny steps. A colleague says this represents getting stuck in a local minimum. What’s the correct interpretation?","options":["A) Tiny steps correspond to a learning rate that is too small, causing extremely slow convergence to the minimum rather than getting stuck in a suboptimal local minimum location","B) The colleague is right: in the fog analogy, tiny steps cause you to settle into the first depression you encounter, which directly corresponds to the local minimum trapping problem","C) The colleague is right: the fog represents noise in the training data, and tiny steps mean the model is too cautious to escape the noise-induced local minimum effectively","D) Tiny steps correspond to the vanishing gradient problem where the slope itself becomes too flat for the optimizer to detect any meaningful direction of descent at all","E) None of the above"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Tiny steps = too-small learning rate = painfully slow convergence, not getting stuck. You’ll eventually reach the minimum but it takes forever. B confuses step size with cost function shape. C describes vanishing gradients (different issue). D misinterprets the fog metaphor."},
{"id":15,"topic":"gradient_descent","topicName":"Gradient Descent","question":"Linear regression’s MSE cost function is convex. A colleague claims this means gradient descent always converges to the optimal solution regardless of the learning rate chosen. What nuance is the colleague missing?","options":["A) Convexity guarantees convergence only when the number of training samples exceeds the number of features; otherwise the cost surface develops flat regions preventing convergence","B) Convexity guarantees a unique global minimum exists but NOT convergence for any learning rate — a rate that is too large causes divergence even on perfectly convex cost functions","C) Convexity only holds when features are standardized; with raw feature scales the cost function can become non-convex due to the elongated contour shapes in parameter space","D) Convexity guarantees convergence to the optimum only when using batch gradient descent; stochastic and mini-batch variants lose the mathematical convexity convergence guarantee","E) None of the above"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Convexity ensures one global minimum but an excessively large learning rate causes divergence (growing oscillation) even on convex surfaces. B is wrong: convexity is a mathematical property unaffected by scaling. C is wrong: convexity is a function property. D is wrong about sample/feature ratio."},
{"id":16,"topic":"gradient_descent","topicName":"Gradient Descent","question":"Training error keeps decreasing. Validation error starts increasing after epoch 50. A colleague recommends reducing the learning rate at epoch 50 instead of applying early stopping. Would this fix overfitting?","options":["A) Yes: a smaller learning rate after epoch 50 prevents the model from making large parameter changes, effectively constraining it from fitting noise in the training data going forward","B) No: the validation error increase indicates a data quality issue beginning at epoch 50, not overfitting, and neither early stopping nor learning rate changes address data quality","C) Yes: learning rate reduction is mathematically equivalent to early stopping because both approaches limit the total magnitude of parameter updates during the training optimization process","D) No: reducing the learning rate slows optimization but does not stop the model from eventually memorizing noise; early stopping at epoch 50 is the correct regularization strategy","E) None of the above"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Reducing the learning rate slows learning but doesn’t stop it — the model still eventually overfits, just more slowly. Early stopping halts training entirely at the best generalization point. B is partially right but the model continues fitting noise. C misdiagnoses. D: they are not equivalent."},
{"id":17,"topic":"gradient_descent","topicName":"Gradient Descent","question":"Update rule: θ_new = θ_old − α·∇J(θ). The gradient ∇J(θ) equals −3.5 at the current θ. A colleague claims θ will decrease because gradient descent always moves parameters toward lower cost values. What actually happens?","options":["A) θ increases: subtracting a negative gradient (−3.5) is equivalent to adding 3.5α, moving θ in the positive direction where the cost function decreases for this parameter","B) θ decreases: the negative sign in the update rule and the negative gradient produce a double negative, which gradient descent resolves by subtracting α times 3.5 from theta","C) θ decreases: gradient descent always decreases parameter values when the cost function has not yet reached its minimum, regardless of the gradient’s sign or magnitude at that point","D) θ stays unchanged: a negative gradient indicates the current position is already at a local minimum, so no meaningful update is applied to the parameter value during this step","E) None of the above"],"correct":0,"explanation":"θ_new = θ_old − α(−3.5) = θ_old + 3.5α. Subtracting a negative = adding. θ increases because the negative gradient tells us cost decreases in the direction of increasing θ. B is wrong: parameters can increase or decrease. C is wrong: negative gradient ≠ minimum. D gets the math wrong."},
{"id":18,"topic":"gradient_descent","topicName":"Gradient Descent","question":"Cost-vs-iteration plot: rapid decrease for 100 iterations, then virtually flat from iteration 100 to 10,000. A colleague says the flat region means the model has started overfitting the training data. Is this correct?","options":["A) Yes: the flat region corresponds to a saddle point where the gradient is zero but the solution is suboptimal, and the model needs momentum-based optimization to escape it effectively","B) No: the flat region indicates the learning rate has decayed to exactly zero, preventing any further parameter updates from occurring regardless of whether the minimum was actually reached","C) No: the flat region indicates the tolerance convergence criterion is met — cost changes are negligibly small, meaning the algorithm found parameters near the minimum of the cost function","D) Yes: when the cost stops decreasing, the model has memorized the training data and further iterations cause test performance to degrade significantly even though training cost stays flat","E) None of the above"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Flat cost = changes between iterations are negligibly small = algorithm has converged (tolerance criterion). This is about optimization convergence, NOT overfitting. B confuses convergence with generalization. C assumes learning rate decay (not stated). D assumes a saddle point without evidence."},
{"id":19,"topic":"bias_variance","topicName":"Bias-Variance Tradeoff","question":"Training accuracy 92%, test accuracy 91%, target 97%. A colleague diagnoses high variance because the model is not performing well enough. What is the actual diagnosis?","options":["A) High variance: the model performs poorly overall which always indicates that the model has memorized noise and is failing to generalize to new observations in the test dataset","B) Both high bias and high variance: the one percent gap shows some overfitting while the five percent shortfall from target shows simultaneous underfitting of the true signal","C) Neither: the model is well-calibrated and the five percent gap to target is caused by irreducible noise in the data that no model can overcome regardless of its complexity level","D) High bias: training and test accuracy are close together indicating low variance, but both are far below the target, meaning the model is too simple to capture the true patterns","E) None of the above"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Key diagnostic: 1% train-test gap = low variance. But both far from 97% = model too simple (high bias). High variance (B) would show a large gap (e.g., 99% train, 80% test). C overcomplicates — 1% gap is negligible variance. D assumes we know the noise level."},
{"id":20,"topic":"bias_variance","topicName":"Bias-Variance Tradeoff","question":"Train accuracy 99.5%, test accuracy 72%. A colleague suggests collecting 5x more data as the primary fix. Is this the most effective first step for closing this performance gap?","options":["A) More data can help with high variance but reducing model complexity or adding regularization would be more direct and cost-effective first steps for this overfitting scenario","B) More data is the best fix because a large train-test gap always indicates insufficient training data rather than a fundamental problem with the model’s complexity level or architecture","C) More data will not help at all because the 99.5% training accuracy proves the model has already extracted every learnable pattern from the currently available training data samples","D) More data is counterproductive here because it would increase the irreducible noise component of the total error decomposition, further reducing the test accuracy performance metric","E) None of the above"],"correct":0,"explanation":"99.5% vs 72% = massive gap = overfitting (high variance). More data CAN help but the most direct fixes target the root cause: reduce complexity, add regularization, dropout. B overgeneralizes. C is wrong: 99.5% often means memorizing noise. D is wrong about irreducible noise."},
{"id":21,"topic":"bias_variance","topicName":"Bias-Variance Tradeoff","question":"Total Error = Bias² + Variance + Noise. A model has low bias but high variance. A colleague proposes adding interaction features (feature₁ × feature₂) to improve test performance. Will this strategy help?","options":["A) No: interaction features only help high bias situations because they address underfitting, but this model already has low bias so they provide absolutely no benefit to the prediction","B) No: adding interaction features increases model complexity which would increase variance further while bias is already low — the exact opposite of what this situation requires","C) Yes: interaction features capture nonlinear relationships that reduce the bias component, and since bias squared decreases faster than variance increases the total error still decreases","D) Yes: interaction features act as implicit regularization because they provide redundant information that helps the model average out noise present in the original individual features","E) None of the above"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Low bias + high variance = model already too complex. Adding interaction features makes it MORE complex, increasing variance. The fix is the opposite: simplify (fewer features, regularization, more data). B’s claim about relative rates is unfounded. C has right conclusion but wrong reasoning. D is incorrect."},
{"id":22,"topic":"bias_variance","topicName":"Bias-Variance Tradeoff","question":"Dart board analogy: predictions are tightly clustered but consistently off-center from the bullseye. A colleague says this is high variance because predictions miss the target by a significant amount. What is correct?","options":["A) Low bias, high variance: the off-center position represents high variance because predictions are far from the true target value regardless of how tightly they are clustered together","B) Low bias, low variance: the tight clustering demonstrates that the model is precise, and the off-center positioning is caused by irreducible noise rather than any model deficiency","C) High bias, high variance: any time predictions consistently miss the target, both error components must be elevated since total error equals bias squared plus variance by definition","D) High bias, low variance: tight clustering means consistent predictions with low variance, while the systematic off-center pattern shows high bias as a persistent directional error","E) None of the above"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Variance = spread of predictions (tight clustering = low variance). Bias = systematic deviation from truth (off-center = high bias). B confuses distance-from-target with variance. C wrongly assumes both must be high. D incorrectly attributes the systematic offset to noise."},
{"id":23,"topic":"bias_variance","topicName":"Bias-Variance Tradeoff","question":"Degree-1: high train AND test error. Degree-4: low train, high test error. Degree-2: moderate train, lowest test error. A colleague says degree-2 will always be optimal for any dataset. Why is this wrong?","options":["A) The colleague is actually correct: quadratic models consistently provide the best bias-variance tradeoff because they add just enough complexity without creating overfitting risk in practice","B) Polynomial degree is irrelevant to the bias-variance tradeoff because the tradeoff is controlled entirely by the regularization parameter lambda rather than by the model structure itself","C) The optimal complexity depends solely on the number of training samples, not the underlying function, so degree-2 would change with different sample sizes but not different data distributions","D) The optimal complexity depends on the specific dataset’s true underlying function; degree-2 is the sweet spot here but different data may require degree one or degree ten or higher","E) None of the above"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The bias-variance tradeoff’s optimal complexity depends on the true underlying function AND dataset. Degree-2 is optimal HERE because the true relationship is roughly quadratic. Different data might need different complexity. B falsely universalizes. C is partially right but incomplete. D is wrong."},
{"id":24,"topic":"bias_variance","topicName":"Bias-Variance Tradeoff","question":"A model has high bias (underfitting). A colleague recommends three actions: (1) collect more data, (2) add polynomial features, (3) reduce regularization strength. Which combination is most effective?","options":["A) All three help: more data gives the model additional patterns to learn, polynomial features capture nonlinear relationships, and reduced regularization allows tighter data fitting simultaneously","B) Actions 1 and 3 help: more data provides evidence to reduce bias and reducing regularization frees the model, while polynomial features risk introducing harmful multicollinearity","C) Actions 2 and 3 address high bias: polynomial features increase model complexity while reducing regularization frees the model to fit more closely; more data helps variance not bias","D) Only action 2 helps: polynomial features directly address underfitting, while more data and regularization changes have no measurable effect on the bias component of the total error","E) None of the above"],"correct":2,"explanation":"For high bias: increase complexity (polynomial features ✔) and reduce regularization (let model fit more ✔). More data does NOT help high bias — a too-simple model stays too simple regardless of data size. B is wrong about data. C ignores regularization’s effect. D is wrong about data helping bias."},
{"id":25,"topic":"regularization","topicName":"Regularization","question":"200 features, only ~10 are suspected to be relevant. A colleague recommends Ridge (L2) as the safe default choice. For this specific sparse scenario, is Ridge the best choice?","options":["A) Yes: Ridge is always the safest default because it shrinks all coefficients proportionally without any risk of accidentally eliminating potentially useful features from the trained model","B) Yes: Ridge outperforms Lasso specifically when the true number of relevant features is small because L2 regularization provides stronger shrinkage on the irrelevant coefficient values","C) No: Lasso (L1) is better here because it drives irrelevant coefficients to exactly zero, performing automatic feature selection when few features are truly relevant in the dataset","D) No: Elastic Net is always superior to both Ridge and Lasso because it combines the advantages of both methods without requiring any additional hyperparameters to tune in practice","E) None of the above"],"correct":2,"explanation":"When you expect sparsity (few relevant features among many), Lasso excels at driving irrelevant coefficients to exactly zero — automatic feature selection. Ridge shrinks but never zeros out. B is reasonable generally but suboptimal for sparse cases. C is wrong: Elastic Net adds a hyperparameter. D inverts the truth."},
{"id":26,"topic":"regularization","topicName":"Regularization","question":"Lasso is applied to data with groups of highly correlated features. It arbitrarily picks one feature per group and zeros the rest, producing unstable results. A colleague suggests switching to Ridge. Is Ridge the best alternative?","options":["A) Ridge is the best alternative because its L2 penalty is everywhere differentiable while Lasso’s L1 penalty has non-differentiable points that cause the arbitrary feature selection behavior","B) Neither Ridge nor Elastic Net helps: when features are highly correlated, you must remove the correlations through PCA before applying any form of regularized regression to the data","C) Elastic Net is better: it combines L1 sparsity with L2’s ability to distribute weights across correlated features, giving stable feature groups instead of arbitrary random selection","D) Ridge is the best alternative because it treats all correlated features equally by shrinking their coefficients to identical values, which produces the most stable and interpretable results","E) None of the above"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Elastic Net is designed for this scenario. L2 component encourages correlated features to share similar coefficients; L1 provides sparsity. Ridge works but loses sparsity. B is misleading: Ridge doesn’t make coefficients identical. C is overkill. D’s reasoning is wrong about the cause."},
{"id":27,"topic":"regularization","topicName":"Regularization","question":"Ridge with λ=1000: J(θ) = MSE + 1000·Σθj². A colleague says this effectively converts the model into Lasso because the coefficients become so small they are essentially indistinguishable from zero. Is this correct?","options":["A) No: Ridge shrinks coefficients toward zero but mathematically never makes them exactly zero; this causes severe underfitting rather than producing feature selection like Lasso does","B) No: large lambda in Ridge causes the model to overfit because the optimizer compensates for the strong penalty by dramatically amplifying the non-penalized bias intercept term","C) Yes: the mathematical equivalence between L1 and L2 norms at extreme regularization strengths means Ridge with large lambda and Lasso produce identical coefficient vectors always","D) Yes: when lambda is sufficiently large in Ridge, the L2 penalty behaves identically to L1, driving coefficients below a numerical threshold that is functionally indistinguishable from zero","E) None of the above"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Ridge’s L2 penalty NEVER produces exact zeros — fundamental mathematical difference from Lasso’s L1. With λ=1000, coefficients are very small (near-zero) but ALL features remain, causing underfitting. B and D state incorrect mathematical equivalences. C inverts the effect on the bias term."},
{"id":28,"topic":"regularization","topicName":"Regularization","question":"Elastic Net: J(θ) = MSE + λ[α·Σ|θj| + (1−α)·Σθj²]. With α=0.5 and λ=10, a colleague claims this equals running Ridge with λ=5 and Lasso with λ=5 separately, then averaging the resulting coefficients. Is this correct?","options":["A) Yes: the linear combination of L1 and L2 penalties in the cost function is mathematically equivalent to training two separate models independently and computing their arithmetic mean","B) Yes: any convex combination of convex penalties can always be decomposed into independent optimization subproblems that are solved separately and then linearly combined afterward","C) No: the equivalence only holds when alpha equals exactly 0.5; for any other alpha value the single-objective and two-model approaches produce different coefficient vectors due to asymmetry","D) No: Elastic Net optimizes both penalties simultaneously in one objective function, producing different coefficients than averaging two independently trained separate regression models","E) None of the above"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Elastic Net jointly optimizes ONE objective where L1 and L2 interact during each gradient step. This is fundamentally different from averaging two separate models. B and D state incorrect mathematical decompositions. C wrongly suggests it works specifically for α=0.5."},
{"id":29,"topic":"regularization","topicName":"Regularization","question":"After Ridge regularization, ‘bathrooms’ coefficient shrinks from 15,000 to 3,500 while ‘sqft’ shrinks from 200 to 180. A colleague concludes sqft is more important because Ridge penalized it less. Is this valid?","options":["A) No: coefficient magnitude depends on feature scale (bathrooms 1–5, sqft 500–5000), so raw shrinkage amounts are not comparable without first standardizing all features to the same scale","B) Yes: Ridge’s L2 penalty applies equal pressure to all coefficients, so the coefficient that changes the least must be the feature the model relies on most for accurate predictions","C) No: the larger shrinkage of bathrooms means Ridge correctly detected it as a noisy feature and is appropriately removing its influence from the model to prevent overfitting behavior","D) Yes: when a coefficient resists shrinkage under L2 regularization, it indicates the corresponding feature has a statistically significant relationship with the target variable being predicted","E) None of the above"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Raw coefficient magnitude depends on feature scale. Bathrooms (1–5) gets 15,000/unit while sqft (500–5000) gets 200/unit. Ridge penalizes squared coefficients, so the large bathroom coefficient is penalized more. Without standardization, shrinkage comparisons are meaningless. B ignores scale. C overinterprets. D conflates with significance."},
{"id":30,"topic":"regularization","topicName":"Regularization","question":"Which of the following statements about regularization techniques is FALSE?","options":["A) Early stopping during gradient descent training acts as a form of regularization by implicitly constraining the effective complexity of the model over the course of training iterations","B) Ridge regression is a reasonable default choice when you have no prior knowledge about which features in the dataset are relevant versus irrelevant for prediction purposes","C) Regularization always improves test performance regardless of the regularization strength parameter lambda that is selected for the model during the hyperparameter tuning process","D) Lasso regression can perform automatic feature selection by driving some feature coefficients to exactly zero during the optimization process based on the L1 penalty term","E) None of the above"],"correct":2,"explanation":"A is FALSE. Too-small λ = no effect (still overfits). Too-large λ = over-regularization (underfits). The optimal λ must be found via cross-validation. B, C, and D are all true statements from the course material."},
{"id":31,"topic":"regularization","topicName":"Regularization","question":"Without regularization: 95% train, 70% test. With optimal L2: 88% train, 86% test. A colleague is alarmed that training accuracy dropped 7 points and wants to reduce regularization strength. Should they?","options":["A) No: the 7-point training drop is expected and desirable — regularization prevents noise memorization, trading training accuracy for much better generalization from 70% to 86% on test data","B) Yes: training accuracy should never decrease when regularization is properly applied because regularization only removes noise from predictions without affecting the model’s ability to fit signal","C) No: but only because the test accuracy improvement is exactly double the training accuracy drop, which indicates the regularization parameter is perfectly calibrated by mathematical definition","D) Yes: a 7-point training accuracy drop indicates regularization strength is too high, and a smaller lambda should preserve training accuracy while still providing some overfitting protection","E) None of the above"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The gap narrowed from 25 points (95-70) to 2 points (88-86). Training accuracy drops because the model can’t memorize noise anymore — this is the intended effect. B wrongly prioritizes training accuracy. C invents a non-existent calibration rule. D is wrong: regularization inherently reduces training fit."},
{"id":32,"topic":"classification","topicName":"Classification","question":"Logistic regression outputs P(default|x) = 0.73 for a loan applicant. A colleague says 0.73 means 73% of similar applicants in the training data defaulted. What does 0.73 actually represent?","options":["A) The model’s estimated probability that this specific applicant will default, computed by passing their individual feature values through the learned sigmoid function, not a training data frequency","B) The proportion of the entire training dataset that belongs to the positive class of defaulters, which serves as the prior probability applied uniformly to all new applicants during prediction","C) The cosine similarity between this applicant’s feature vector and the average feature vector of all defaulters in the training set, normalized to fall within the range of zero to one","D) The normalized distance from this applicant’s feature values to the logistic regression decision boundary, scaled by the sigmoid function to produce a value between zero and one","E) None of the above"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Logistic regression computes P(y=1|x) = 1/(1+e^(−θᵀx)) for each individual’s specific features. The 0.73 is a probability for THIS applicant, not a training data frequency (B), cosine similarity (C), or distance (D)."},
{"id":33,"topic":"classification","topicName":"Classification","question":"A colleague uses MSE as the cost function for logistic regression, arguing it worked for linear regression. The model converges to a poor solution. Why did MSE fail while log-loss succeeds?","options":["A) MSE penalizes all prediction errors with equal intensity regardless of the model’s confidence level, while log-loss provides much stronger gradient signals for confidently wrong classification predictions","B) MSE combined with the sigmoid function creates a non-convex cost surface with multiple local minima; log-loss remains convex with the sigmoid, guaranteeing convergence to the global optimum","C) MSE requires continuous target variables to compute mathematically defined and numerically stable gradients, and binary classification labels violate this requirement causing undefined gradient values","D) MSE cannot handle binary target variables because squaring the difference between predictions and binary labels of zero and one produces gradient values outside the valid probability range","E) None of the above"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Sigmoid + MSE = non-convex surface with local minima where GD gets stuck. Sigmoid + log-loss = convex surface. C describes a secondary benefit of log-loss but not the primary failure reason. B is wrong: MSE can compute on binary targets. D is wrong: gradients are defined for binary targets."},
{"id":34,"topic":"classification","topicName":"Classification","question":"KNN: k=1 gives 100% training accuracy but poor test accuracy. k=50 gives lower training AND poor test accuracy. A colleague says k=1 is better because it has perfect training performance. Why is this fundamentally flawed?","options":["A) The comparison is invalid because KNN with k=1 and k=50 are fundamentally different algorithm families that cannot be compared using the same accuracy metric or evaluation framework","B) The colleague’s reasoning is actually correct: higher training accuracy always correlates with better generalization performance when the algorithm family is kept constant across experiments","C) Both values give poor test accuracy which conclusively proves KNN is inappropriate for this specific dataset regardless of the k value, and a different algorithm family should be used","D) Training accuracy is irrelevant for model selection; k=1 memorizes data causing overfitting while k=50 oversmooths boundaries causing underfitting, so the optimal k balances both effects","E) None of the above"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Model selection must prioritize TEST performance. 100% training accuracy with k=1 is just memorization. k=50 oversmooths. Optimal k (e.g., 5–15) balances variance (small k) and bias (large k). B is wrong: same algorithm. C is premature. D is fundamentally wrong about the accuracy-generalization relationship."},
{"id":35,"topic":"classification","topicName":"Classification","question":"A node has 60 samples class A, 40 class B. A colleague computes Gini as 1 − (0.6 + 0.4) = 0.0 and concludes the node is pure. What computational error did they make?","options":["A) They should have used entropy instead of Gini because Gini impurity is only mathematically valid for multiclass classification problems with three or more classes, not binary problems","B) They forgot to square the class proportions: correct Gini = 1 − (0.6² + 0.4²) = 1 − (0.36 + 0.16) = 0.48, indicating substantial impurity rather than a pure node","C) They used the wrong formula entirely: Gini impurity uses the product of proportions (0.6 × 0.4 = 0.24) not their sum, and the result must be doubled to get the final impurity value","D) They applied the formula correctly but misinterpreted what zero means: a Gini value of exactly 0.0 indicates maximum impurity in binary classification scenarios, not a perfectly pure node","E) None of the above"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Gini = 1 − Σ(p_k²). The colleague forgot to SQUARE the proportions. Correct: 1 − (0.36 + 0.16) = 0.48, far from pure. B describes a different formula. C is wrong: 0.0 Gini IS pure (0.5 is maximum for binary). D is wrong: Gini works for binary classification."},
{"id":36,"topic":"classification","topicName":"Classification","question":"Gmail used Naive Bayes for spam detection despite the ‘naive’ independence assumption being violated (‘Nigerian’ and ‘prince’ are correlated in spam). A colleague says NB works because modern spam actually has independent word distributions. Is this correct?","options":["A) Yes: statistical analysis of modern spam emails confirms that word independence holds approximately, which explains why Naive Bayes achieves near-optimal classification performance on email data","B) No: Gmail uses a modified Naive Bayes variant that explicitly models pairwise word correlations through bigram features added during the text preprocessing and feature extraction pipeline stage","C) Yes: the independence assumption is satisfied after applying TF-IDF weighting to the raw word features, which effectively decorrelates the word features before passing them to the Bayes classifier","D) No: NB works despite violated independence because classification only needs correct probability rankings between classes, not perfectly calibrated absolute probability values for each class","E) None of the above"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Spam words ARE correlated. NB works because for classification you only need P(spam|x) > P(not-spam|x) to be correct — the ranking is usually preserved even with miscalibrated probabilities from violated independence. B is factually wrong. C invents a non-existent modification. D is wrong: TF-IDF doesn’t decorrelate."},
{"id":37,"topic":"classification","topicName":"Classification","question":"A depth-20 decision tree has leaf nodes with 1–2 samples and 99.9% training accuracy but 62% test accuracy. A colleague worries that pruning will permanently lose information captured by the deep branches. Is this concern valid?","options":["A) No: but only because decision trees can be regrown to the original depth at any time from the stored training dataset, so pruning is always a perfectly reversible operation in practice","B) Yes: each split in a decision tree represents a statistically significant pattern that the algorithm validated during training, so removing any split discards confirmed useful information","C) No: deep branches with 1–2 samples captured noise not signal; setting max_depth and min_samples_per_split removes overfitted splits while retaining genuine patterns in the upper tree","D) Yes: deep tree branches capture rare but genuine edge cases that shallow trees inevitably miss, so pruning always sacrifices model performance on minority patterns in the test dataset","E) None of the above"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Leaf nodes with 1–2 samples are memorizing individual examples (noise), not genuine patterns. Pruning removes these while keeping informative splits. B romanticizes deep branches. C’s reasoning (reversibility) is beside the point. D is wrong: tree splits are NOT individually tested for statistical significance."},
{"id":38,"topic":"classification","topicName":"Classification","question":"Softmax outputs [0.05, 0.10, 0.70, 0.10, 0.05] for 5 classes. A colleague claims this is equivalent to running 5 independent sigmoid classifiers. Why is this incorrect for mutually exclusive classes?","options":["A) Softmax is designed exclusively for binary classification problems where it mathematically reduces to the sigmoid function, while independent sigmoids properly handle the multiclass classification case","B) Softmax and independent sigmoids produce mathematically identical outputs because the exponential normalization in softmax is algebraically equivalent to applying sigmoid to each class independently","C) Softmax normalizes outputs across all classes so they sum to exactly 1.0, forming a valid probability distribution; independent sigmoids have no such constraint and can sum to any arbitrary value","D) Softmax outputs are not true probabilities but rather unbounded confidence scores that can exceed 1.0 in certain edge cases, unlike sigmoid which is always strictly bounded between zero and one","E) None of the above"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Softmax: P(k) = e^(s_k)/Σe^(s_i), ensuring ΣP(k)=1.0. Five independent sigmoids could output [0.8, 0.9, 0.3, 0.7, 0.6] summing to 3.3 — not a valid distribution for mutually exclusive classes. B is mathematically wrong. C inverts the usage. D is wrong: softmax IS bounded and does sum to 1."},
{"id":39,"topic":"classification","topicName":"Classification","question":"Linear regression predicts spam probability as −0.3 for one email and 1.4 for another. A colleague says these can be thresholded at 0.5 for classification, making logistic regression unnecessary. What is the fundamental problem?","options":["A) Values outside [0,1] can be clipped to the valid range by setting negatives to zero and values above one to one, which makes linear regression equivalent to logistic regression after correction","B) Values outside [0,1] are invalid probabilities, the decision boundary shifts unpredictably with outlier inputs, and the cost function is not designed for classification optimization at all","C) The only problem is computational efficiency: linear regression requires more iterations to converge than logistic regression for binary classification tasks, but final results are equivalent","D) Linear regression can actually be used for classification by thresholding, and in practice it produces results identical to logistic regression when the classes are well-separated in feature space","E) None of the above"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Outputs of −0.3 and 1.4 aren’t probabilities. Sigmoid constrains to (0,1). Thresholding linear regression is problematic: uncalibrated outputs, boundary shifts with outliers, wrong cost function. B is wrong: different results. D: clipping doesn’t fix underlying issues. C is wrong: not equivalent."},
{"id":40,"topic":"ensemble","topicName":"Ensemble Methods","question":"A fintech company builds a credit scoring model. A single decision tree achieves 78% accuracy with clear interpretability that regulators appreciate. The team switches to a random forest (100 trees) achieving 89% accuracy, but a regulator asks: \"How does each tree in the forest differ from the single tree, and why can't you just use 100 copies of the same best tree?\" The correct technical explanation AND its implication for interpretability are:","options":["A) Each tree trains on a bootstrap sample (with replacement) and considers random feature subsets at each split, creating diverse trees that make different errors; however, this diversity makes the ensemble a black box since no single tree represents the full model logic","B) Each tree uses a different splitting criterion (some use Gini, others entropy, others variance reduction) selected randomly, creating algorithmic diversity; this makes the ensemble harder to audit since the splitting logic varies","C) Each tree is trained sequentially on the errors of the previous tree, so later trees correct earlier mistakes; this sequential dependency means the first tree is interpretable but later trees only make sense in the context of all previous ones","D) Each tree trains on a completely different subset of customers with no overlap, so the 100 trees collectively see 100x more data than one tree; the interpretability loss is minimal because each tree is still individually interpretable","E) None of the above"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Random forest creates diversity through TWO mechanisms: (1) bootstrap sampling (same-size samples WITH replacement from the same data, NOT different subsets), and (2) random feature selection at each split. This diversity means trees make uncorrelated errors, and averaging reduces variance. But the ensemble loses the single-tree interpretability that regulators value. B incorrectly claims 100x more data. C invents varying criteria. D describes boosting, not bagging."},
{"id":41,"topic":"ensemble","topicName":"Ensemble Methods","question":"A hospital deploys a random forest to predict patient readmission risk. During a regulatory audit, the team claims they validated the model using out-of-bag (OOB) error and found 87% accuracy. The auditor asks whether OOB error is a legitimate substitute for a held-out test set, and whether the team should have used stratified cross-validation instead given that only 8% of patients are readmitted. The correct assessment of BOTH the OOB validity AND the stratification concern is:","options":["A) OOB error is valid and the class imbalance concern is irrelevant because bootstrap sampling automatically creates balanced samples by oversampling the minority class proportionally in each bootstrap draw","B) OOB error is a valid generalization estimate because each sample is predicted only by trees that never trained on it; however, the auditor is right that with 8% readmission rate, OOB evaluation may not maintain class proportions across individual tree evaluations, potentially making the OOB estimate unreliable for the minority class","C) OOB error is invalid because the bootstrap samples overlap with each other, creating data leakage between the trees that inflates the OOB estimate; stratified CV with completely independent folds eliminates this leakage","D) OOB error is invalid because it evaluates each sample using only a fraction of the trees rather than the full ensemble, underestimating true performance; stratified CV would use the complete forest for every evaluation fold","E) None of the above"],"correct":1,"explanation":"OOB error IS valid - each sample's prediction comes from ~1/3 of trees that never saw it, closely matching CV estimates. But the auditor raises a legitimate concern: with 8% readmission, individual OOB evaluations per sample may have high variance for the minority class. B is wrong: OOB using fewer trees is a feature (honest evaluation), not a bug. C is wrong: bootstrap does NOT oversample minorities. D is wrong: bootstrap overlap is not data leakage."},
{"id":42,"topic":"ensemble","topicName":"Ensemble Methods","question":"A Mastercard fraud detection team tests two ensemble approaches on transaction data. Random Forest achieves 94% F1 on fraud detection with stable performance across quarterly retraining cycles. Gradient Boosting achieves 96% F1 initially but degrades to 88% F1 after retraining on new data containing a fraud ring with unusual patterns. The team lead attributes the GBM degradation to \"the algorithm being inherently unstable.\" The actual technical explanation for GBM's degradation AND why RF was more stable is:","options":["A) GBM's sequential training focused later trees heavily on the unusual fraud ring patterns (hard examples), overfitting to those specific patterns; RF's independent parallel trees with random subsets naturally regularize against overfitting to any single pattern subset","B) GBM degraded because gradient boosting cannot handle concept drift in sequential data, while random forests are specifically designed for non-stationary distributions through their bootstrap sampling mechanism","C) GBM degraded because each new tree must be compatible with all previous trees in the sequence, creating architectural constraints that prevent adaptation; RF trees are independent and can each adapt freely to new patterns","D) GBM degraded because its learning rate was set too high for the new data distribution, while RF has no learning rate parameter and is therefore immune to this type of hyperparameter sensitivity across data shifts","E) None of the above"],"correct":0,"explanation":"GBM's sequential error correction means later trees focus on residuals (hard cases). When the fraud ring creates unusual hard cases, GBM overfits to them. RF's independent trees with random feature/sample subsets create diversity that prevents overfitting to any specific pattern subset. B oversimplifies - learning rate matters but isn't the root cause. C is wrong - neither is specifically designed for concept drift. D mischaracterizes GBM's architecture."},
{"id":43,"topic":"ensemble","topicName":"Ensemble Methods","question":"A data science team at an e-commerce company runs a Kaggle-style internal competition. Team A uses standard Gradient Boosting (scikit-learn's GradientBoostingClassifier). Team B uses XGBoost with identical hyperparameters (same max_depth, n_estimators, learning_rate). Team B consistently achieves 1-2% higher accuracy AND trains 5x faster. Team A claims this is unfair because \"XGBoost is just a faster implementation of the same algorithm.\" The technical reasons Team A's claim is INCORRECT and the specific XGBoost features that explain BOTH the accuracy and speed differences are:","options":["A) XGBoost achieves higher accuracy by using deeper trees by default and achieves faster training by distributing tree construction across multiple machines using a map-reduce architecture that standard GBM does not support","B) XGBoost adds L1 and L2 regularization on leaf weights (preventing overfitting that standard GBM suffers from), handles missing values natively (avoiding information loss from imputation), and parallelizes tree construction at the split-finding level (not tree-level parallelism)","C) XGBoost is mathematically identical to standard GBM but uses GPU acceleration and SIMD instructions for faster matrix operations; the accuracy difference comes solely from numerical precision improvements in the gradient computation","D) XGBoost uses a fundamentally different tree structure called \"boosted random forests\" that combines bagging within each boosting iteration, creating a hybrid ensemble that is both more accurate and more parallelizable than pure boosting","E) None of the above"],"correct":1,"explanation":"XGBoost is NOT the same algorithm as standard GBM. Key differences: (1) built-in L1+L2 regularization on leaf weights (standard GBM has none), (2) native missing value handling, (3) second-order gradient approximation, (4) parallel split-finding (within each tree, not across trees). B invents \"boosted random forests.\" C fabricates default depths and map-reduce. D wrongly claims mathematical identity."},
{"id":44,"topic":"ensemble","topicName":"Ensemble Methods","question":"A bank builds a credit default model using stacking: base models are logistic regression (LR), random forest (RF), and gradient boosting (GBM). The meta-learner is a logistic regression trained on base model predictions. During development, a junior data scientist trains the meta-learner using the base models' predictions on the TRAINING data. The model achieves 95% accuracy in development but only 82% in production. The root cause of this 13-point performance drop AND the correct methodology to prevent it are:","options":["A) The base models' training-set predictions are overfit (especially GBM and RF which memorize training data), so the meta-learner learned to trust inflated prediction quality; the fix is using out-of-fold (k-fold) predictions for the meta-learner's training data","B) The base models perform differently in production because the data distribution shifted; the fix is retraining all base models on the most recent data and implementing continuous monitoring for distribution drift","C) The training predictions from LR, RF, and GBM are too correlated because they all learned the same patterns from the same training data; the fix is training each base model on a different random subset to ensure prediction diversity","D) The meta-learner overfits because logistic regression has too many parameters when trained on three base model outputs; the fix is replacing the logistic regression meta-learner with a simple averaging function that requires no training","E) None of the above"],"correct":0,"explanation":"When base models predict on their own training data, GBM (99%+ train accuracy) and RF (similar) produce overly confident, overfit predictions. The meta-learner then learns to heavily weight these overfit signals. In production, base models produce honest (lower-quality) predictions, and the meta-learner can't handle this gap. Out-of-fold predictions simulate production conditions during meta-learner training. B oversimplifies. C misdiagnoses the root cause. D doesn't fix the leakage."},
{"id":45,"topic":"ensemble","topicName":"Ensemble Methods","question":"An insurance company builds two ensemble models for claim fraud detection. Model A (Random Forest, 500 trees) achieves: train AUC=0.96, test AUC=0.93. Model B (AdaBoost, 500 rounds) achieves: train AUC=0.99, test AUC=0.84. The chief actuary observes Model B's higher training AUC and argues it is \"learning the patterns better\" and should be deployed with more training data to close the gap. The flaw in this reasoning AND the architectural explanation for Model B's larger train-test gap are:","options":["A) Model B overfits because 500 rounds is too many for AdaBoost; the solution is to reduce to fewer than 50 rounds, since AdaBoost's theoretical optimal number of rounds is always less than the square root of the training set size","B) Model B's 99% train AUC indicates overfitting not better learning; AdaBoost's sequential reweighting mechanism increasingly focuses on hard-to-classify samples including mislabeled or noisy claims, effectively memorizing outliers that don't generalize","C) Model B's train-test gap is caused by AdaBoost using decision stumps which are too simple for fraud patterns; switching to depth-3 trees as base learners would reduce variance while maintaining the same training AUC level","D) Model B's higher training AUC is expected because AdaBoost uses a more expressive loss function than Random Forest; the test gap would close with more training data because AdaBoost's bias decreases faster than its variance increases with sample size","E) None of the above"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The 15-point train-test gap (0.99 vs 0.84) in AdaBoost is classic overfitting. AdaBoost reweights samples each round, increasingly focusing on hard cases. If these include noise/outliers, later rounds memorize them. RF's independent trees with averaging are inherently more robust. B incorrectly claims more data always fixes boosting overfitting. C invents a square-root rule. D misdiagnoses the cause (stumps vs deep trees is about bias, not the variance issue shown here)."},
{"id":46,"topic":"neural_networks","topicName":"Neural Networks","question":"A 10-layer network with sigmoid activation: early layers stop learning while later layers learn normally. A colleague diagnoses this as the early layers overfitting to low-level features. What is the actual problem?","options":["A) Vanishing gradients: sigmoid’s maximum gradient is 0.25, so multiplying through 10 layers gives approximately 10⁻⁶, making early layer weight updates negligible; ReLU activation fixes this","B) Exploding gradients: sigmoid amplifies gradients through deep layers causing numerical overflow in early layer computations, which prevents stable weight updates during backpropagation training","C) Dead neurons: sigmoid outputs saturate at zero or one for most inputs in early layers, causing the neurons to become permanently inactive and unable to contribute during the training process","D) Early layers are overfitting to low-level features like edges and textures while later layers correctly learn high-level patterns, creating an imbalance in learning speed across the network","E) None of the above"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Vanishing gradient: sigmoid max gradient = 0.25. Through 10 layers via chain rule: 0.25¹⁰ ≈ 10⁻⁶. Early layer gradients are negligible. ReLU (gradient=1 for positive) fixes this. B mischaracterizes the problem. C describes the OPPOSITE (exploding). D describes a ReLU issue (dying ReLU), not sigmoid."},
{"id":47,"topic":"neural_networks","topicName":"Neural Networks","question":"3 output classes (cat, dog, bird). A colleague proposes 3 neurons with independent sigmoid activations instead of softmax, noting both produce values in (0,1). Why is softmax preferred for mutually exclusive classes?","options":["A) Softmax produces more accurate predictions because the exponential function in its formula amplifies differences between class scores more effectively than the sigmoid logistic curve does","B) Independent sigmoids cannot produce valid outputs for more than two classes because the sigmoid function is mathematically defined only for binary classification decision boundary computations","C) Softmax normalizes outputs to sum to exactly 1.0 forming a valid probability distribution; independent sigmoids can produce outputs summing to any value, violating the mutual exclusivity constraint","D) Independent sigmoids are computationally more expensive than softmax because each sigmoid requires a separate forward pass through the entire network during the prediction computation phase","E) None of the above"],"correct":2,"explanation":"For mutually exclusive classes, probabilities must sum to 1. Softmax enforces this. Three independent sigmoids might output [0.8, 0.9, 0.3] summing to 2.0 — nonsensical. B is wrong: sigmoids are computationally simpler. C overstates. D is wrong: sigmoid can be applied to any number of outputs."},
{"id":48,"topic":"neural_networks","topicName":"Neural Networks","question":"Task A: classify product images. Task B: predict stock prices from historical sequences. A colleague suggests RNNs for both because ‘pixels can be read sequentially left-to-right, giving images a sequential structure.’ Why is this wrong?","options":["A) RNNs can effectively process images by reading pixels sequentially, but they are computationally too slow for production image classification systems because of sequential computation bottlenecks","B) Images have 2D spatial structure best captured by CNNs with convolutional filters; time series have temporal dependencies best captured by RNNs; reading pixels sequentially discards spatial locality","C) The colleague is correct for images but wrong for stocks: RNNs detect sequential patterns in pixel rows effectively, while stock prices require convolutional networks to detect temporal price patterns","D) Both tasks should exclusively use transformer architectures because transformers have completely replaced both CNNs and RNNs across all domains including computer vision and time series analysis","E) None of the above"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Images have 2D spatial locality (nearby pixels relate). CNNs exploit this with convolutional filters. Sequentializing pixels destroys this structure. Time series genuinely have sequential dependencies for RNNs/LSTMs. B cites speed, not the fundamental mismatch. C inverts the pairing. D overgeneralizes."},
{"id":49,"topic":"neural_networks","topicName":"Neural Networks","question":"A colleague explains backpropagation as ‘rerunning the forward pass with corrected weights to gradually improve predictions.’ What does backpropagation actually compute and why is this description wrong?","options":["A) It re-executes the forward pass multiple times with slightly perturbed weights to numerically estimate the gradient of the loss function for each individual weight in the entire network","B) It compares current predictions against stored copies of previous iteration predictions to determine which weights improved performance and which weights degraded the model’s output quality","C) It computes gradients of the loss with respect to each weight by propagating error signals backward through layers using the chain rule, enabling gradient descent to update all weights","D) It propagates the correct output labels backward through the network layers to directly compute the optimal value each weight should take, bypassing iterative gradient-based optimization entirely","E) None of the above"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Backpropagation = computing ∂Loss/∂w for every weight using the chain rule, working backward from output. These gradients enable gradient descent updates. B describes numerical gradient estimation (much slower). C describes direct solution (infeasible for neural networks). D describes nothing in standard training."},
{"id":50,"topic":"neural_networks","topicName":"Neural Networks","question":"Single-layer perceptron cannot solve XOR. A colleague argues it could with ReLU activation instead of step function, since ReLU introduces nonlinearity. Is this correct?","options":["A) No: single-layer computes a linear combination then applies activation; no activation function can overcome the linear boundary limitation without adding a hidden layer for nonlinear transformation","B) No: but a single layer with sigmoid activation could solve XOR because the sigmoid function’s smooth S-shaped curve can approximate any arbitrarily complex decision boundary given enough neurons","C) Yes: ReLU introduces piecewise nonlinearity that allows a single-layer perceptron to create the two separate linear decision boundaries needed to correctly separate the XOR class labels","D) Yes: any universal approximator activation function applied in a single computational layer can represent any boolean function including XOR given a sufficient number of neurons in that layer","E) None of the above"],"correct":0,"explanation":"A single-layer perceptron computes y=f(Σwᵢxᵢ+b). The weighted sum is always linear. ANY activation applied to a single linear combination cannot create a nonlinear decision boundary needed for XOR. A hidden layer provides the necessary nonlinear transformation BEFORE classification. B, C, D all incorrectly claim a single layer suffices."},
{"id":51,"topic":"neural_networks","topicName":"Neural Networks","question":"LSTM vs vanilla RNN: a colleague says LSTMs are superior simply because they have more parameters per cell, giving them greater memory capacity for storing sequence information. What is the actual innovation?","options":["A) LSTMs use self-attention mechanisms that allow each time step to directly access information from all other time steps, completely bypassing the sequential processing bottleneck of vanilla RNNs","B) LSTMs have more parameters per cell which provides greater raw memory capacity, allowing them to store and recall information from significantly longer sequences than vanilla RNN architectures","C) LSTMs process sequences in both forward and backward directions simultaneously, giving them access to future context that vanilla RNNs cannot utilize due to their unidirectional architectural design","D) LSTMs use gating mechanisms (forget, input, output gates) that selectively control information flow, allowing relevant information to persist across long sequences and mitigating vanishing gradients","E) None of the above"],"correct":3,"explanation":"LSTMs’ innovation is gates: forget gate (what to discard), input gate (what to store), output gate (what to expose). These create a gradient highway preventing vanishing gradients. B oversimplifies — it’s not just more parameters. C describes bidirectional RNNs (separate concept). D describes transformer attention (different architecture)."},
{"id":52,"topic":"neural_networks","topicName":"Neural Networks","question":"A team has a structured tabular dataset with 10,000 samples and 50 features for a classification task. A colleague insists on using a deep neural network because 'deep learning always outperforms traditional methods.' The team's XGBoost baseline achieves 92% accuracy. What is wrong with the colleague's reasoning?","options":["A) The 92% XGBoost accuracy proves the dataset has a fundamental performance ceiling that no algorithm can exceed, making the switch to deep learning pointless regardless of architecture chosen","B) Deep neural networks always outperform XGBoost on any dataset given sufficient hyperparameter tuning, so the colleague is correct but the team needs to tune the neural network more carefully first","C) Deep learning cannot process tabular data at all because convolutional and recurrent layers require spatial or sequential structure that flat feature tables fundamentally do not provide","D) On structured tabular data with moderate sample sizes, gradient boosting methods like XGBoost frequently match or outperform deep neural networks, which excel primarily on unstructured data like images and text","E) None of the above"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Multiple large-scale benchmarks (e.g., Grinsztajn et al., 2022) show that gradient boosting (XGBoost, LightGBM) frequently matches or outperforms deep learning on tabular data, especially with moderate sample sizes. Deep learning excels on unstructured data (images, text, audio) where it can exploit spatial/sequential patterns. B is false — this is an empirical fact, not a tuning issue. C invents a ceiling. D is wrong — MLPs can process tabular data, they just often do not outperform boosting."},
{"id":53,"topic":"model_evaluation","topicName":"Model Evaluation","question":"A European bank deploys a fraud detection model and reports 99.5% accuracy to the board. Only 0.5% of transactions are fraudulent. The compliance officer is satisfied, but the fraud investigation team discovers that the model catches virtually no actual fraud — legitimate transactions flagged as fraud are almost zero, but so are detected frauds. The accuracy figure that satisfied the board AND the metric that reveals the model's actual failure are:","options":["A) The model achieves 99.5% accuracy because it learned to flag the 0.5% of fraudulent transactions correctly while also correctly classifying all legitimate ones; the low detection rate is caused by a threshold set too high in production","B) A naive model predicting \"not fraud\" for every transaction achieves the same 99.5% accuracy because 99.5% of transactions are legitimate; recall on the fraud class (near 0%) reveals the model detects almost no actual fraud despite high accuracy","C) The 99.5% accuracy is inflated by the bank's oversampling strategy during training which artificially balanced the classes; precision on the fraud class would reveal that most fraud alerts are actually legitimate transactions","D) The 99.5% accuracy is calculated on the training set where the model memorized the fraud patterns; the test accuracy would be significantly lower because fraud patterns change rapidly and the model cannot generalize","E) None of the above"],"correct":1,"explanation":"This is the accuracy paradox. With 99.5% legitimate transactions, always predicting \"not fraud\" gives 99.5% accuracy while catching ZERO fraud. The board was fooled by a meaningless metric. Recall (what fraction of actual frauds are caught?) exposes the failure. B speculates about oversampling without evidence. C assumes the issue is train vs test. D contradicts the stated scenario (model catches virtually no fraud)."},
{"id":54,"topic":"model_evaluation","topicName":"Model Evaluation","question":"Cancer screening: precision=0.95, recall=0.40. A colleague prioritizes improving precision because ‘false alarms waste hospital resources.’ In this medical screening context, is this the right priority?","options":["A) No: recall should be prioritized because missing 60% of actual cancer cases through false negatives is far more dangerous than ordering unnecessary follow-up tests through false positives","B) Yes: improving precision reduces the psychological harm to patients who are incorrectly told they might have cancer, which is considered the primary harm in cancer screening program design","C) No: neither metric should be prioritized individually; the F1 score should be maximized because it treats false positives and false negatives as equally costly across all medical applications","D) Yes: hospital resources are severely limited, so reducing false positives through improved precision ensures diagnostic resources are allocated efficiently to patients most likely to have cancer","E) None of the above"],"correct":0,"explanation":"In cancer screening, missed diagnosis (false negative) can mean delayed treatment and death. False alarm (false positive) means additional testing — inconvenient but not fatal. With recall=0.40, 60% of cancers are MISSED. B and D prioritize convenience over lives. C ignores that FP and FN costs differ enormously in healthcare."},
{"id":55,"topic":"model_evaluation","topicName":"Model Evaluation","question":"Model A: AUC=0.92. Model B: AUC=0.78. A colleague says ‘Model A is 14% more accurate.’ What are TWO things wrong with this statement?","options":["A) AUC differences are not additive because the ROC curve is plotted on a logarithmic scale, so 0.92 minus 0.78 does not equal a 0.14 improvement in discrimination capability between models","B) AUC measures discrimination ability across all thresholds not accuracy, and 0.14 AUC points is not the same as a 14% relative improvement in any meaningful classification performance metric","C) The comparison is invalid because AUC can only be meaningfully compared between models trained on the exact same algorithm family, not across different model architectures or feature sets used","D) AUC of 0.92 means the model correctly classifies 92% of all samples while 0.78 means 78% correct, so the true difference is 14 percentage points of accuracy not 14 percent relative","E) None of the above"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Two errors: (1) AUC ≠ accuracy — AUC = probability of ranking a random positive above a random negative. (2) 0.14 AUC units ≠ ‘14%.” B is wrong: ROC uses linear axes. C is wrong: AUC CAN compare across models. D directly states the AUC=accuracy misconception."},
{"id":56,"topic":"model_evaluation","topicName":"Model Evaluation","question":"A colleague uses 10-fold cross-validation and reports 90% average accuracy. They conclude 'since we used CV, our model cannot be overfit.' What is fundamentally wrong with this reasoning?","options":["A) Cross-validation detects overfitting by estimating generalization error, but it does not prevent it; if hyperparameters were tuned using the CV score directly, the reported 90% is optimistically biased","B) Cross-validation only prevents overfitting when the number of folds equals the number of training samples, which is known as leave-one-out cross-validation and is the only unbiased variant","C) Cross-validation cannot detect overfitting at all because it trains on the same underlying dataset in every fold, making it equivalent to evaluating on the training set with extra computation","D) Cross-validation eliminates overfitting entirely because each fold uses a separate validation set, ensuring the model never sees the same data during training and evaluation simultaneously","E) None of the above"],"correct":0,"explanation":"CV is a DIAGNOSTIC tool that estimates generalization error — it does not prevent overfitting. If you use CV scores to select hyperparameters and then report those same CV scores, you get optimistic bias (need nested CV). The model itself can still overfit within each fold. B wrongly claims CV eliminates overfitting. C invents a rule about LOO. D goes too far — CV does detect overfitting, it just does not prevent it."},
{"id":57,"topic":"model_evaluation","topicName":"Model Evaluation","question":"Rare disease: 2% prevalence, 200 samples (≈4 positive cases). A colleague uses regular 5-fold CV. What specific failure mode can occur compared to stratified k-fold in this extreme imbalance scenario?","options":["A) Regular k-fold would automatically oversample the minority class within each fold to ensure balanced class representation, which introduces artificial data that biases the performance estimate","B) Class imbalance makes k-fold cross-validation theoretically invalid regardless of stratification because the folds cannot maintain statistical independence with so few positive case samples","C) Regular k-fold places all four positive cases in the same fold, which improves that fold’s evaluation accuracy but leaves the other four folds with no positive samples to evaluate against","D) A fold might contain zero positive cases, making training on that fold impossible and evaluation meaningless; stratified k-fold ensures each fold preserves the original 2% class distribution ratio","E) None of the above"],"correct":3,"explanation":"With only 4 positives in 5 folds, regular k-fold might put all positives in 1–2 folds, leaving others with ZERO (can’t compute recall, precision, etc.). Stratified ensures each fold gets ~1 positive. B partially identifies the problem. C is wrong: stratified CV is valid. D is wrong: k-fold doesn’t automatically oversample."},
{"id":58,"topic":"model_evaluation","topicName":"Model Evaluation","question":"Precision=0.75, Recall=0.50. A colleague computes the arithmetic mean: (0.75+0.50)/2=0.625 and says ‘F1 must be wrong since it gives 0.60 not 0.625.’ Why is F1 deliberately lower than the arithmetic mean?","options":["A) F1 subtracts a correction factor for class imbalance from the arithmetic mean, which accounts for the base rate of positive cases in the dataset during the final metric calculation step","B) F1 uses the harmonic mean 2PR/(P+R) which is always less than or equal to the arithmetic mean and penalizes imbalance: it rewards balanced precision and recall over one high and one low","C) F1 uses the geometric mean square root of P times R, which gives a lower result than the arithmetic mean whenever precision and recall differ, incentivizing models to equalize both metric values","D) F1 is computed as the minimum of precision and recall which equals 0.50, but adds a bonus of 0.10 for the precision-recall gap being less than 0.30, giving the adjusted final value of 0.60","E) None of the above"],"correct":1,"explanation":"F1 = 2PR/(P+R) = 2(0.75)(0.50)/(1.25) = 0.60. Harmonic mean ≤ arithmetic mean, with the gap increasing when values differ. This PENALIZES imbalance. B: geometric mean would give ≈0.61. C and D describe nonexistent formulas."},
{"id":59,"topic":"model_evaluation","topicName":"Model Evaluation","question":"House price prediction: $10K error on a $100K house vs $10K error on a $1M house. A colleague uses MAE, arguing ‘a dollar error is a dollar error regardless of the house’s value.’ Why might MAPE be more appropriate?","options":["A) MAE is biased toward expensive houses because their residuals are naturally larger in absolute terms, while MAPE eliminates this systematic bias by normalizing errors against the predicted values","B) A $10K error represents 10% of a $100K house but only 1% of a $1M house; MAPE captures this relative significance which better reflects the actual economic impact of prediction errors","C) MAE fundamentally cannot handle heteroscedastic data where variance changes with the target value level, while MAPE automatically corrects for heteroscedasticity through its percentage normalization","D) MAPE should always be used instead of MAE for all regression problems because percentage errors are universally more interpretable than absolute errors across all domains and target value ranges","E) None of the above"],"correct":1,"explanation":"$10K on $100K house (10%) is much more significant than on $1M house (1%). MAPE normalizes by actual value. B is partially right but normalizes by actual, not predicted. C overgeneralizes: MAPE fails near zero. D: MAE doesn’t ‘handle’ heteroscedasticity but MAPE doesn’t ‘correct’ it either."},
{"id":60,"topic":"model_evaluation","topicName":"Model Evaluation","question":"A data scientist evaluates on the test set, tunes hyperparameters, re-evaluates on the same test set, repeats until reaching 91% accuracy. A colleague says this is fine since ‘the test data was never used for training.’ What subtle leakage is occurring?","options":["A) No leakage occurs because test data was never included in gradient computations during backpropagation, which is the only mechanism through which information can leak from test data into model weights","B) This iterative process is actually the standard recommended practice and is explicitly documented in scikit-learn’s official documentation as the correct workflow for model evaluation and selection","C) The leakage is negligible because hyperparameter tuning only changes high-level model properties like learning rate and regularization rather than directly modifying individual weight parameter values","D) Each evaluate-and-tune cycle leaks test set information into model selection decisions; the reported 91% is optimistically biased because the test set implicitly guided hyperparameter choices","E) None of the above"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Each evaluate-tune cycle uses test performance to guide decisions, effectively ‘fitting’ to the test set through model selection. The 91% is optimistically biased. Correct workflow: validation set or CV for tuning, test set ONCE at the end. B defines leakage too narrowly. C underestimates impact. D is false."},
{"id":61,"topic":"clustering","topicName":"Clustering","question":"K-means with K=4 gives different clusters every run. A colleague suggests increasing K to 10 for more stability, arguing more clusters means less sensitivity to initialization. Does increasing K fix the instability?","options":["A) Yes: with more clusters each centroid captures a smaller more homogeneous group of points, which reduces the sensitivity to initial centroid placement and produces consistent results across runs","B) No: instability comes from random centroid initialization, not K; K-means++ initialization or running multiple restarts and selecting the lowest inertia result fixes the root cause directly","C) Yes: K=10 approaches the number of natural groups in most datasets, and as K matches the true cluster count the algorithm converges to the same stable solution regardless of initialization","D) No: increasing K actually increases instability because there are exponentially more possible configurations, giving the algorithm even more local minima to converge to unpredictably each run","E) None of the above"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Instability = random initialization, not K. K-means++ (spreading initial centroids) or multiple restarts (picking lowest inertia) fix the root cause. Increasing K doesn’t fix initialization sensitivity. B’s logic is flawed. C is partially right but overstates. D assumes K=10 matches true structure."},
{"id":62,"topic":"clustering","topicName":"Clustering","question":"Tuscany tourist mobility data: some clusters are spherical (Florence), others elongated (coastline). K-means fails on coastal clusters. A colleague suggests K-means with Manhattan distance. Would this fix the shape problem?","options":["A) Yes: any non-Euclidean distance metric fundamentally changes K-means from a centroid-based algorithm to a density-based algorithm, enabling it to discover non-spherical cluster shapes automatically","B) No: but hierarchical clustering with single linkage would properly solve this because single linkage naturally creates chain-like elongated clusters by joining the two closest individual data points","C) Yes: Manhattan distance creates diamond-shaped decision boundaries that can approximate elongated clusters significantly better than the circular boundaries produced by Euclidean distance metrics","D) No: K-means assumes spherical clusters regardless of distance metric because it assigns points to nearest centroids; DBSCAN discovers arbitrary shapes based on local density patterns instead","E) None of the above"],"correct":3,"explanation":"K-means’ fundamental assumption is spherical clusters (minimizes distance to centroids). Changing distance metric changes shape slightly (circles→diamonds) but not arbitrary shapes. DBSCAN groups by density, handling ANY shape. B: diamonds aren’t arbitrary shapes. C: single linkage has problems. D is completely wrong."},
{"id":63,"topic":"clustering","topicName":"Clustering","question":"Elbow method: inertia drops sharply K=2→4, then gradually K=4→10. A colleague argues K=10 is optimal because it achieves the absolute lowest inertia value. Why is minimizing inertia not the correct objective?","options":["A) Inertia always decreases monotonically with K reaching zero at K=N, so minimizing it always selects K=N which is one point per cluster and completely meaningless; the elbow identifies diminishing returns","B) Minimizing inertia is actually the mathematically correct objective, and the elbow method is an outdated heuristic that has been fully replaced by the gap statistic in all modern clustering analysis","C) Inertia cannot be meaningfully compared across different values of K because the metric’s absolute scale changes with each additional cluster, making direct cross-K numerical comparisons statistically invalid","D) Inertia is systematically biased toward solutions with equal-sized clusters, so minimizing it forces an artificial balance between cluster sizes that may not reflect the true underlying data structure","E) None of the above"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Inertia monotonically decreases as K increases (at K=N, inertia=0). Minimum inertia always picks K=N — useless. The elbow finds where the RATE of decrease drops sharply = diminishing returns. B is a concern but not why minimizing is wrong. C: inertia CAN be compared. D: elbow is still widely used."},
{"id":64,"topic":"clustering","topicName":"Clustering","question":"A data point has silhouette score −0.2. A colleague says this definitively identifies the point as an outlier that should be removed from the dataset before re-running the clustering algorithm. Is this interpretation correct?","options":["A) Yes: negative silhouette scores below the threshold of negative 0.1 are the standard criterion for identifying outliers in clustering analysis, and such points should always be removed before reclustering","B) Yes: the negative score indicates the point lies in an overlapping region between two cluster probability distributions, which means it contains noise that would contaminate both clusters if retained","C) No: silhouette scores mathematically cannot be negative because they measure the ratio of intra-cluster to inter-cluster distance, and distance ratios are always positive values by mathematical definition","D) No: a negative silhouette score means the point is closer to a neighboring cluster than its own, suggesting misassignment to the wrong cluster rather than necessarily being an outlier data point","E) None of the above"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Silhouette = (b−a)/max(a,b), range [−1,+1]. Negative means closer to neighboring cluster (b<a) — likely MISASSIGNED, not necessarily an outlier. Reassigning it might improve the score. B invents a threshold. C is wrong: the formula produces negatives. D conflates misassignment with noise."},
{"id":65,"topic":"clustering","topicName":"Clustering","question":"DBSCAN labels some points as noise. A colleague argues this is a disadvantage because ‘every data point represents a real customer and we’re losing valuable business data.’ In what important scenario is noise detection actually critical?","options":["A) When outliers carry meaningful information such as fraud, manufacturing defects, or network intrusions, DBSCAN identifies them naturally while K-means forces them into the nearest cluster distorting centroids","B) Noise detection is only useful when the dataset contains more than 10% outliers; below this threshold the noise points have negligible impact on cluster quality and should simply be included in clusters","C) DBSCAN’s noise detection feature is only relevant when using DBSCAN as a preprocessing step for K-means, identifying points to systematically remove before the actual clustering algorithm runs on clean data","D) Noise detection is always a disadvantage in practical business scenarios because outliers often represent rare but highly valuable customer segments that carry disproportionately high lifetime business value","E) None of the above"],"correct":0,"explanation":"In anomaly detection (fraud, defects, intrusions), the ‘noise’ points ARE the interesting data. K-means forces outliers into clusters, distorting centers and hiding anomalies. DBSCAN naturally separates them. B invents a threshold. C overgeneralizes. D mischaracterizes DBSCAN’s role."},
{"id":66,"topic":"clustering","topicName":"Clustering","question":"A business analyst wants customer segments at both 2-cluster and 6-cluster granularity from one analysis. A colleague suggests running K-means twice with K=2 and K=6. What is the limitation compared to hierarchical clustering?","options":["A) Running K-means twice requires exactly double the computation, while hierarchical clustering is always strictly faster because it only needs a single run to produce all possible cluster granularity levels","B) Two K-means runs produce independent unrelated solutions; hierarchical clustering’s dendrogram shows how six fine clusters nest within two broad clusters, preserving the hierarchical relationship","C) K-means cannot be run with different K values on the same dataset because the algorithm modifies the underlying data during its iterative training process, altering the input for subsequent runs","D) K-means with K=2 and K=6 produces identical cluster assignments because K-means always converges to the same underlying data structure regardless of the number of clusters specified in the input","E) None of the above"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Two K-means runs are independent — the 2-cluster split may not align with how the 6-cluster solution groups data. Hierarchical clustering builds a nested tree where 6 clusters are sub-groups of 2. B is wrong. C: hierarchical is often SLOWER than K-means. D: K-means doesn’t modify data."},
{"id":67,"topic":"dim_reduction","topicName":"Dimensionality Reduction","question":"5,000 features: a model using all features performs worse than one using only 50 features. A colleague diagnoses multicollinearity. While that may exist, what is the more fundamental explanation?","options":["A) The curse of dimensionality: in high-dimensional spaces data becomes extremely sparse, distance metrics lose discriminative power, and models overfit to noise in uninformative features","B) The model with 5,000 features is underfitting because it spreads its parameter budget across too many features, giving insufficient learning capacity per individual feature to detect meaningful patterns","C) The 50-feature model benefits from implicit regularization because having fewer features forces the optimizer to find the minimum-norm solution that automatically generalizes better to new data","D) Multicollinearity is the correct and complete diagnosis: correlated features inflate coefficient variance so dramatically that the model’s predictions become entirely unstable on unseen test data","E) None of the above"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Curse of dimensionality: (1) sparsity, (2) distance concentration, (3) overfitting to noise in many uninformative dimensions. Multicollinearity (B) is ONE symptom, not the root cause. C inverts the problem: too many features causes overfitting, not underfitting. D is partially true but doesn’t explain why 5,000 features hurts."},
{"id":68,"topic":"dim_reduction","topicName":"Dimensionality Reduction","question":"PCA on 100 features: first 5 PCs explain 95% of total variance. A colleague keeps only these 5 for a classification task, arguing 'we preserved 95% of the information so classification accuracy should be unaffected.' What is the flaw in this reasoning?","options":["A) Keeping only 5 PCs always reduces classification accuracy proportionally to the variance lost, so the model will be exactly 5% less accurate than using all original features","B) PCA maximizes variance which is unsupervised; the discarded 5% of variance might contain the most discriminative features for separating classes in the classification task","C) PCA components are nonlinear transformations of the original features, so they cannot be used as direct inputs to linear classifiers without first applying an inverse transformation","D) The 95% variance threshold is too aggressive; the standard practice requires retaining at least 99% of the total variance before any dimensionality reduction can be applied safely","E) None of the above"],"correct":1,"explanation":"PCA maximizes VARIANCE, which is unsupervised and unrelated to class separability. A direction with tiny variance could be the one that best separates classes. For example, if two classes differ on a feature with low variance, PCA might discard it. This is why supervised methods (LDA) or evaluating classification performance after PCA are important. B invents a proportional rule. C is wrong: PCA IS linear. D invents a threshold."},
{"id":69,"topic":"dim_reduction","topicName":"Dimensionality Reduction","question":"PCA shows no clusters in 2D; t-SNE reveals clear groups. A colleague concludes t-SNE is always superior for visualization tasks. What critical caveat makes this conclusion problematic?","options":["A) t-SNE is actually a supervised method that uses class labels to create visually separated clusters, so its clear groups are a consequence of injected label information rather than genuine data structure","B) t-SNE can only reduce data to exactly two dimensions while PCA can reduce to any arbitrary number of dimensions, making PCA strictly more flexible for downstream machine learning tasks","C) t-SNE preserves local neighborhoods but distorts global distances and cluster sizes, so apparent visual separations can be artifacts; PCA’s linear projection faithfully preserves global variance structure","D) t-SNE results are fully deterministic and reproducible while PCA results depend on random initialization, making PCA more sensitive to the specific random seed used during the computation process","E) None of the above"],"correct":2,"explanation":"t-SNE distorts global structure: inter-cluster distances and cluster sizes are NOT meaningful. Two clusters appearing far apart might actually be close. PCA preserves global relationships faithfully. B is wrong: t-SNE is unsupervised. C inverts reality: t-SNE is non-deterministic. D: t-SNE can reduce to any dimensionality."},
{"id":70,"topic":"dim_reduction","topicName":"Dimensionality Reduction","question":"Swiss roll: 2D manifold in 3D. Dropping the z-axis squashes layers together. A colleague says PCA would avoid this because it finds the ‘optimal’ projection that maximizes information preservation. Why is PCA also insufficient?","options":["A) PCA finds the best linear projection, but the Swiss roll has nonlinear structure; unrolling requires nonlinear manifold learning methods like t-SNE or Isomap that respect geodesic distances on the surface","B) PCA would work perfectly on the Swiss roll because the first two principal components capture the unrolled 2D surface, since PCA maximizes variance which naturally aligns with the unrolling direction","C) PCA fails specifically because it requires the data to follow a normal Gaussian distribution, and the Swiss roll has a uniform distribution on the manifold surface which violates this essential assumption","D) PCA cannot handle 3D input data; it is limited to datasets with more than ten dimensions because eigendecomposition becomes numerically unstable for small covariance matrices in low-dimensional spaces","E) None of the above"],"correct":0,"explanation":"PCA finds the LINEAR subspace maximizing variance. For the Swiss roll, max variance cuts ACROSS the rolled layers, squashing them. Unrolling requires understanding NONLINEAR manifold structure. B is wrong: PCA’s linear projection squashes. C: PCA doesn’t require normality. D: PCA works in any dimensionality."},
{"id":71,"topic":"dim_reduction","topicName":"Dimensionality Reduction","question":"PCA’s first PC has high loadings on income, credit_limit, and spending. A colleague says these three features are redundant and two should be removed from the model entirely. Does PCA support this?","options":["A) Yes: high loadings on the same principal component is the standard statistical test for feature redundancy, and features loading together should be reduced to the single one with the highest loading","B) Not necessarily: but the features should be decorrelated through orthogonal rotation before modeling because PCA only identifies redundancy without actually addressing it in the original feature space","C) Yes: if three features load heavily onto one component they contain identical information by mathematical definition, and keeping all three triples the same signal in any downstream prediction model","D) Not necessarily: high loadings on the same PC means shared variance, but each feature may contribute unique information on other PCs; PCA captures their shared signal without discarding any feature","E) None of the above"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Shared variance (high loadings on same PC) = correlation, not exact redundancy. Each feature may contribute unique variance on other PCs. PCA’s composite captures shared signal without needing to discard features. B overstates loadings as a redundancy test. C misapplies rotation. D: correlated ≠ identical."},
{"id":72,"topic":"recommender","topicName":"Recommendation Systems","question":"Netflix: new user, only 2 movies watched. CF fails. A colleague suggests showing globally popular movies until more data accumulates. What advantage does content-based filtering have over this popularity approach?","options":["A) Popularity-based recommendations require real-time computation of global viewing statistics across the entire platform, which is more computationally expensive than content-based filtering for small user profiles","B) Globally popular movies have already been watched by most users on the platform, so recommending them provides no incremental value and increases the rate of new user churn during onboarding","C) Content-based filtering leverages the genres and features of those 2 watched movies to personalize recommendations immediately, rather than ignoring the user’s demonstrated preference signal entirely","D) Showing only popular movies creates a reinforcing feedback loop where popular content becomes even more popular, eventually reducing the diversity of content the platform produces and acquires","E) None of the above"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The user already signaled preferences by watching 2 movies. Content-based filtering analyzes those movies’ features (genre, actors, style) to personalize immediately. Popularity ignores this signal. B is speculative. C: popularity stats are trivially pre-computed. D is a real long-term concern but not the immediate problem."},
{"id":73,"topic":"recommender","topicName":"Recommendation Systems","question":"Amazon: ‘Customers who bought X also bought Y.’ A colleague says this is user-based CF because it explicitly mentions ‘customers.’ Why is this actually item-based collaborative filtering?","options":["A) It computes item-item co-occurrence similarity based on which items are frequently purchased together, not user-user similarity; the word customers describes the data source not the similarity type","B) It is a hybrid approach that cannot be classified as either user-based or item-based because it simultaneously computes both similarity types and combines them in a weighted ensemble model","C) It is actually content-based filtering because the system analyzes product attributes like category, brand, and price to find items with similar feature profiles to the item being purchased","D) It is user-based CF as the colleague states: the system finds users similar to the current buyer and recommends items those similar users purchased, leveraging the customer purchase history directly","E) None of the above"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Despite mentioning ‘customers,’ the computation is item-item: which items are co-purchased. User-based CF would be ‘users similar to you liked Y.’ The phrase describes item co-occurrence aggregated across ALL customers, not user similarity. B takes the phrase at face value. C ignores co-purchase signal. D overcomplicates."},
{"id":74,"topic":"recommender","topicName":"Recommendation Systems","question":"Spotify’s filter bubble: only recommends songs similar to current listening habits. A colleague suggests fixing this by randomly inserting songs from genres the user has never explored. What is the problem with pure random exploration?","options":["A) Random song insertion is the mathematically optimal strategy because it guarantees maximum diversity, which research consistently shows is the single primary driver of long-term user engagement metrics","B) Random exploration only works for new users during onboarding; for established users with clear and stable preference patterns, the system should exclusively exploit known preferences to maximize satisfaction","C) Random exploration risks alienating users with irrelevant content; multi-armed bandit algorithms balance exploitation of known preferences with intelligent exploration of novel but likely enjoyable content","D) Random insertion of unfamiliar songs is computationally expensive because it requires maintaining a separate index of all songs the user has never interacted with, growing linearly with catalog size","E) None of the above"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Random exploration is too aggressive — death metal for a jazz lover wastes recommendations and annoys users. Multi-armed bandits use INFORMED exploration: try content with some probability of matching latent interests, learning from responses. B overstates diversity. C is too extreme. D: tracking unheard songs is trivial."},
{"id":75,"topic":"recommender","topicName":"Recommendation Systems","question":"User-item matrix: 10M users, 100K movies, 99.9%+ entries empty. A colleague says CF cannot work due to insufficient data density. Is this assessment correct?","options":["A) Yes: collaborative filtering requires at least 10% matrix density to compute reliable user or item similarities, and at 0.1% density the signal-to-noise ratio is far too low for meaningful recommendations","B) No: matrix factorization decomposes the sparse matrix into low-rank user and item factor matrices, discovering latent features that enable predicting missing entries despite extreme sparsity levels","C) Yes: the only viable approach for this extreme level of sparsity is pure content-based filtering because it relies on item features rather than user-item interactions and is unaffected by sparsity","D) No: but the correct approach is filling missing entries using mean imputation before applying CF, which converts the sparse problem into a dense matrix that collaborative filtering handles easily","E) None of the above"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Matrix factorization (SVD, ALS) handles extreme sparsity by finding low-rank approximations. Users×K and K×Items matrices discover latent factors from available ratings and predict missing ones. B invents a density threshold. C: mean imputation destroys signal. D: CF with matrix factorization works — the Netflix Prize proved this."},
{"id":76,"topic":"recommender","topicName":"Recommendation Systems","question":"A content-based book recommender only suggests sci-fi to a sci-fi reader. A colleague proposes adding more sophisticated NLP features analyzing writing style and themes. Would better features solve the overspecialization problem?","options":["A) No: overspecialization can only be solved by completely removing the genre feature from the content representation, forcing the system to recommend based on other attributes that naturally cross genres","B) Yes: sophisticated features capturing deep narrative structure would reveal cross-genre similarities, such as a sci-fi novel and historical fiction sharing similar storytelling patterns and thematic complexity","C) No: overspecialization is inherent to content-based filtering regardless of feature quality; only collaborative filtering discovers cross-genre interests through users with overlapping but diverse taste profiles","D) Yes: NLP analysis of writing style would reveal that all books fundamentally share the same narrative patterns, effectively making every book similar to every other book and eliminating genre boundaries entirely","E) None of the above"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Better features might find some cross-genre similarities (B has a point), but the fundamental limitation remains: content-based filtering can ONLY recommend items similar to what the user has seen. CF finds cross-genre interests through similar users’ diverse tastes. C: removing genre alone doesn’t fix structural limitation. D is absurd."},
{"id":77,"topic":"explainability","topicName":"Model Explainability","question":"GDPR: a customer’s loan is denied by a neural network. A colleague proposes showing the customer the model’s global feature importance rankings as the explanation. Why is this insufficient for GDPR compliance?","options":["A) Global feature importance fully satisfies GDPR requirements because the regulation only requires showing which features the model considers important, not explaining specific individual decision outcomes","B) GDPR does not apply to neural network models specifically because the regulation was drafted before deep learning became mainstream and has not been updated to explicitly cover these architectures","C) Neither global nor local explanations satisfy GDPR because the regulation mandates that all automated decisions be made exclusively by inherently interpretable models such as logistic regression","D) GDPR requires explaining this specific decision, not general model behavior; LIME provides local explanations by fitting an interpretable model around the individual denied application instance","E) None of the above"],"correct":3,"explanation":"GDPR’s right to explanation requires explaining why THIS person was denied, not how the model works generally. LIME generates local explanations specific to the individual case. B misinterprets GDPR. C is wrong: GDPR doesn’t mandate model types. D is wrong: GDPR applies to all automated decisions."},
{"id":78,"topic":"explainability","topicName":"Model Explainability","question":"SHAP values for an employee: salary=−0.15, commute=+0.25, tenure=−0.10, overtime=+0.20. A colleague interprets commute’s +0.25 as meaning ‘the employee has a 25-minute commute.’ What does +0.25 actually represent?","options":["A) The commute feature pushed this employee’s predicted turnover probability 0.25 above the average prediction; it quantifies the feature’s contribution to this specific prediction, not the feature’s raw value","B) The commute feature has a 25% probability of being the single most important feature for this specific prediction; SHAP values represent the likelihood that each feature is the primary outcome driver","C) The commute feature would change the overall prediction by exactly 0.25 if it were completely removed from the model; SHAP values measure the impact of feature ablation on each individual prediction","D) The employee’s commute distance is 0.25 standard deviations above the mean commute in the training data; SHAP normalizes all feature values to z-scores before computing their prediction contribution","E) None of the above"],"correct":0,"explanation":"SHAP decomposes predictions into feature contributions: prediction = base_value + Σ(SHAP). +0.25 means commute pushed this employee’s turnover prediction 0.25 above average. It’s a contribution to the PREDICTION, not the feature’s value (A), probability (B), z-score (C), or ablation impact (D)."},
{"id":79,"topic":"explainability","topicName":"Model Evaluation","question":"Permutation importance: shuffling ‘age’ drops accuracy from 88% to 72% (16% drop). Shuffling ‘purchase_count’ drops from 88% to 87% (1% drop). A colleague says age must be causing overfitting since the model depends on it so heavily. Is this correct?","options":["A) No: the 16% drop actually indicates that age is merely a proxy variable for other correlated features, and the model’s apparent reliance on it masks the true importance of those underlying features","B) Yes: permutation importance exceeding 10% is the standard threshold for identifying features that cause overfitting, and such features should be regularized or completely removed from the model","C) Yes: when a single feature causes such a dramatic accuracy drop, the model has overspecialized on that feature and would generalize better if the feature were removed or its influence were reduced","D) No: high permutation importance means the model relies on that feature for correct predictions, indicating genuine predictive signal, not overfitting; diagnosing overfitting requires train-test comparison","E) None of the above"],"correct":3,"explanation":"High permutation importance = the model NEEDS that feature for accurate predictions = genuine predictive power, NOT overfitting. Overfitting is diagnosed by comparing train vs test performance, not by feature importance. B incorrectly equates reliance with overfitting. C is possible but not what the colleague claimed. D invents a threshold."},
{"id":80,"topic":"explainability","topicName":"Model Explainability","question":"A colleague uses LIME (local, model-agnostic) to generate a global feature importance ranking by averaging LIME explanations across all predictions. Another colleague says this is invalid since ‘LIME is local not global.’ Who is right?","options":["A) The second colleague is right: aggregating local explanations violates the locality assumption that LIME’s linear surrogate model is only accurate within a small neighborhood around each individual prediction","B) The first colleague is right but should use SHAP instead, because SHAP is the only method whose mathematical foundations formally support both local explanation and global aggregation of explanations","C) The first colleague’s approach is valid: aggregating many local LIME explanations can produce meaningful global insights, similar to how SHAP summary plots aggregate local SHAP values across all instances","D) The second colleague is right: LIME explanations are computed on perturbed local neighborhoods that never overlap, so averaging them produces mathematically meaningless values across the entire dataset","E) None of the above"],"correct":2,"explanation":"LIME IS designed for local explanations, but aggregating them across many instances is valid — similar to SHAP summary plots. D raises a real concern about locality but doesn’t invalidate aggregation (you’re aggregating many local results, not extrapolating one). B is wrong. C: LIME aggregation is also valid."},
{"id":81,"topic":"explainability","topicName":"Model Explainability","question":"From the course’s explainability summary table: Decision Trees are intrinsic with global and local scope. LIME is model-agnostic with local scope only. Permutation Importance is model-agnostic with global scope only. Which method is model-agnostic AND provides both global and local explanations?","options":["A) SHAP, which works with any model type and provides both individual prediction explanations through local waterfall plots and aggregated feature importance summaries through global summary plots","B) Decision Trees, because they can be applied as surrogate models for any black-box model, making them effectively model-agnostic while retaining their natural global and local interpretability properties","C) LIME, because while designed for local explanations, its perturbation-based approach can be trivially extended to produce global explanations by increasing the perturbation neighborhood size parameter","D) Permutation Importance, because shuffling features can be applied locally to individual predictions and globally across the entire dataset to provide both levels of explanation simultaneously","E) None of the above"],"correct":0,"explanation":"From the lecture table: SHAP = model-agnostic, global & local. A: Permutation Importance is global only. B: Decision Trees are intrinsic (not model-agnostic). D: LIME’s extension to global isn’t trivial and it’s still classified as local. SHAP is the only method checking all boxes."},
{"id":82,"topic":"responsible_ai","topicName":"Responsible AI","question":"Amazon’s hiring AI trained on 10 years of male-dominated resumes penalized ‘women’s chess club’ and all-women’s colleges. Removing the gender feature did not fix the bias. A colleague says the remaining bias must be an algorithm bug. Why is this wrong?","options":["A) The algorithm is functioning correctly and the observed bias is actually desirable because it reflects genuine differences in qualification patterns between male and female engineering candidates historically","B) Bias persists through proxy features like college names, activities, and word choice that correlate with gender; removing the explicit feature does not remove implicit gender encoding throughout the data","C) The bias persists because the algorithm was not retrained after removing the gender feature column; simply deleting a column requires complete model retraining to eliminate the feature’s learned influence","D) The remaining bias is not actually gender-related; it is a coincidental pattern from the specific ten-year data window that happens to correlate with gender but has no causal relationship to it","E) None of the above"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Classic failure of ‘fairness through unawareness.’ Gender is encoded in proxies: all-women’s colleges, ‘women’s chess club,’ writing style. The model learned these from biased data. B is ethically and legally wrong. C: even with retraining, proxies persist. D is implausible given systematic discrimination."},
{"id":83,"topic":"responsible_ai","topicName":"Responsible AI","question":"A bank uses a 50M-parameter neural network for credit scoring. Under GDPR, a customer requests an explanation for their denial. A colleague suggests training a global decision tree surrogate model. What limitation does this have?","options":["A) Global surrogate models are explicitly prohibited under GDPR because they provide an approximate explanation of an approximate model, creating a chain of approximations that regulators consider insufficient","B) A global surrogate approximates overall behavior but may not faithfully represent this specific decision; LIME or SHAP explain the actual model’s individual prediction more accurately for the customer","C) A decision tree surrogate would perfectly satisfy GDPR compliance requirements because the regulation only requires that an explanation be provided in some generally understandable format for consumers","D) The surrogate approach is fundamentally invalid because a decision tree cannot approximate a neural network with fifty million parameters; the approximation error would be far too large for practical use","E) None of the above"],"correct":1,"explanation":"A global surrogate mimics the neural network ON AVERAGE but may disagree on specific predictions. The customer needs an explanation of THEIR denial, not the model’s general behavior. LIME/SHAP explain the actual model’s specific decision. B: no such prohibition. C oversimplifies GDPR. D: trees CAN approximate at high level."},
{"id":84,"topic":"responsible_ai","topicName":"Responsible AI","question":"Healthcare AI: AUC=0.95 for white patients, AUC=0.75 for non-white patients. A colleague suggests adding ‘race’ as a feature so the model can learn race-specific patterns and equalize performance. What is wrong with this approach?","options":["A) Adding race would fix the performance gap because the model could learn separate optimized decision boundaries for each racial group, equalizing predictions within each demographic category independently","B) Adding race would have no measurable effect because the model has already implicitly learned all racial patterns through proxy features like zip code, insurance type, and healthcare utilization history","C) Adding race risks encoding direct racial discrimination into the model; the correct fix is collecting more representative training data for underrepresented groups to equalize performance organically","D) The performance gap is acceptable because AUC of 0.75 still exceeds the standard clinical threshold of 0.70 that healthcare regulators require for all demographic subgroups in AI system deployment","E) None of the above"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Using race directly creates discriminatory infrastructure and legal liability. The root cause is underrepresentation in training data. Collecting diverse data addresses this. B might improve metrics but creates discriminatory models. C invents a regulatory threshold and ignores a massive 0.20 AUC gap. D might be partially true but doesn’t justify adding race."}];
